#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078374 :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957" :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127 :topic/math-academy-id 876 :topic/title "Exponential Growth and Decay Models With First-Order ODEs"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0731f6-6530-57d1-8101-d583dce27200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238171} {:answer/id #uuid "30cfb7a5-89a3-5976-92cb-41428186daaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238479} {:answer/id #uuid "afd882b4-452c-52f4-9d6a-2ad9a9e823b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239582} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0874aa-3afc-56be-b514-dbaa87daff29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239832} {:answer/id #uuid "d2980861-6b7b-5001-a205-b1b4d310530b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30cfb7a5-89a3-5976-92cb-41428186daaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238479} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b7af48-1a47-5f7e-aa94-0ab3a1a923bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240670}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fb765b0-7266-558a-8445-d5e7e8dcfb07" :question/math-academy-id "q-105890" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=2,a=500,$ and ${y}_{0}=600.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=400<a=500.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e3800c8-cb88-5193-ac78-ecad629bac8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800y - 20y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075107} {:answer/id #uuid "cc86f27c-9102-5471-8151-edb2db7a3851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 20y^{2} - 800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075108} {:answer/id #uuid "de0a15f9-0ee2-5d22-9af1-22fcb5891d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 - 20y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075109} {:answer/id #uuid "34c6d6ee-08e6-51ad-b478-11481d79e6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 + 20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075110} {:answer/id #uuid "6537327b-2aa9-5b4f-8d32-77ba1961661c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 - 20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075111}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c6cb1533-9217-5c12-ab91-a8596f52e7b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075106}] :question/id #uuid "efe4332d-8d00-5d94-9cfa-87582b5e92ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-105893" :question/problem "Given that $y > 40$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc34311-544b-58ac-b0d4-5ffec4e304fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80(y+20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238574} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20476d-f9bb-5130-8f9e-628e39957f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80y(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238728} {:answer/id #uuid "565d5a3d-b07c-5417-aca5-0f14a0b69ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238800} {:answer/id #uuid "90d5ff36-f97d-5e89-b7ec-2b83f4fa6dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80y(y+20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239310} {:answer/id #uuid "e37d7ad7-c270-5174-be15-143df61c1f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id